[00:36.43]Conversation One
[00:38.29]W: Hey, Mario. 
[00:39.68]Can you tell me how many people are living in Australia today£¿
[00:43.70]M: There are more than 15 million people living in Australia. 
[00:48.05]But only 160,000 of these are natives.
[00:52.14]W: So where have the rest come from£¿ 
[00:54.54]M: Well, until 1850 most of the settlers came from Britain and Ireland and, as we know, many of these were prisoners. 
[01:03.67]Then in 1851 something happened and changed everything. 
[01:08.57]Gold was discovered in southeastern Australia. 
[01:12.28]During the next ten years, nearly 700,000 people went to Australia to find gold and become rich.
[01:20.29]Many of them were Chinese. 
[01:22.28]W: When did the first Italians arrive in Australia£¿
[01:25.91]M: The first Italians went there, like the Chinese, in the gold-rush, hoping to find gold and become rich. 
[01:32.89]But most also went there for political reasons. 
[01:36.96]During the 1850s and 1860s different states in Italy were fighting for independence and some Italians were forced to leave their homelands because they were in danger of being put in prison for political reasons.
[01:51.50]W: I believe there are a lot of Italians in the sugar industry.
[01:55.36]M: Yes, that's right. In 1891 the first group of 300 Italians went to work in the sugarcane fields of northern Australia. 
[02:04.75]They worked very hard and many saved enough money to buy their own land. 
[02:09.71]In this way they came to dominate the sugar industry on many parts of the Queensland coast.
[02:15.46]W: But not all Italians work in the sugar industry, do they£¿
[02:19.10]M: No. A lot of them are in the fishing industry. 
[02:22.38]Italy has a long coastline, as you know, and Italians have always been good fishermen. 
[02:28.56]At the end of the 19th century some of them went to Western Australia to make a new life for themselves. 
[02:34.74]Again, many of them, including my grandfather, were successful.
[02:39.51]W: Wow, you are really a walking history textbook. 
[02:42.93]Thank you for telling me all those things.
[02:45.15]M: My pleasure.
[02:47.11]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[02:52.12]1. What does the speaker say about the population in Australia£¿
[03:11.39]2. Why did most of the first Italians go to Australia£¿
[03:29.54]3. How did Italians dominate the sugar industry on many parts of the Queensland coast£¿
[03:50.15]4. What do we learn about Italians according to the conversation£¿
[04:08.64]Conversation Two
[04:10.62]W: Hey, Tom. 
[04:11.58]Do you really believe that clothes carry a kind of message for other people, and that what we put on is in some way a reflection of what we feel£¿
[04:20.06]M: Oh, yes, I think so. 
[04:21.80]People are beginning now to take seriously the idea of a kind of psychology of clothing, to believe that there is not just individual taste in our clothes but also thinking behind what we wear, which is trying to express something we may not even be aware of ourselves.
[04:37.68]W: Surely this has always been the case. 
[04:40.04]We all dress up when we want to impress someone, such as for a job interview with a prospective employer. 
[04:46.37]We tend to make an effort and put on something smart.
[04:49.51]M: True, but that's a conscious act. 
[04:51.60]What I'm talking about is more of a subconscious thing. 
[04:54.52]Take the student who is away from home at college or university for example: if he tends to wrap himself up more than others, he is probably feeling homesick. 
[05:03.28]W: Can you give any other examples of this kind£¿
[05:06.19]M: Yes. I think people who are sociable and outgoing tend to dress in an extrovert way, preferring brighter or more dazzling colors¡ªyellow, bright red, and so on. 
[05:15.65]In the same way, what might be seen as a parallel with the animal kingdom, aggressive clothes might indicate an aggressive personality or attitude towards life. 
[05:24.08]Think about the threat displays used by animals when they want to warn off opponents.
[05:28.25]W: Do you think the care over the way we actually wear our clothes has anything to tell us£¿
[05:32.98]M: Yes, indeed. 
[05:34.25]The length, for example, of a man's trousers speaks volumes about his awareness of his own image. 
[05:39.19]Or, to give you other examples, minority groups often tend to express themselves by wearing unconventional or what some might consider outrageous clothing, as a way of showing their thoughts and feelings are different from the rest, and so they find an outlet in this way.
[05:53.46]W: That makes sense. 
[05:54.80]Well, thank you for explaining that to me.
[05:57.18]M: Not at all.
[05:59.33]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[06:04.24]5. What are the speakers talking about£¿
[06:21.64]6. Why do people dress up when they have a job interview£¿
[06:39.35]7. What kind of people like yellow or bright red clothes according to the man£¿
[07:00.28]8. What does the man say about the length of a man's trousers£¿
