[10:36.32]Passage Two
[10:37.97]There's no doubt that the computer has enlarged man's working capacity as well as his intellectual capacity enormously. 
[10:46.07]But it brings with it dangers to match the benefits. 
[10:49.83]Now by this, I mean the dangers to physical and mental well-being of the people who work at computer terminals, not the dangers to personal privacy or national or industrial security.
[11:02.50]There's a very alarming set of statistics which comes from a survey done in the UK on 800 pregnant women, who happened to use computer terminals for a major part of their working days.
[11:14.62]In no less than 36% of the subjects there were some severe abnormalities during the pregnancy, enough to make a termination necessary. 
[11:24.23]Now these figures compare significantly with a control group of pregnant women of the same age who did not work with computer terminals. 
[11:33.38]The rate of severe abnormalities in their case was only 16%. This survey confirms similar investigations carried out in Denmark, Canada, Australia and the US.
[11:45.84]Ironically, it seems that it's not only those who work with computers are at risk. 
[11:51.11]There's perhaps more danger for people who use computers for interest or pleasure in their own homes. 
[11:56.92]Most people get so fascinated by what they are doing that they stay in front of the screen for hours.
[12:03.24]Now, no one yet has a clear idea about the exact connection between working or playing with computer terminals and the problems with physical and mental well-being, but the figures at least suggest that there's a cause for alarm.
[12:18.47]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[12:23.39]12. What is the passage mainly about£¿
